From: NanoKVM build <nbatkins@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: phy: add a driver for the Sophgo CV1800B internal EPHY

The SG2002's internal 10/100 EPHY comes out of reset shut down, with its
analog front end powered off and untuned and with no identification, and
needs the CVITEK vendor bring-up sequence before it will train a link.

That sequence used to run in U-Boot (meta-sophgo's 0004 patch), which left
the front end -- line driver, PLL, RX ADC and PGA -- powered for the whole
of boot. Move it here so it runs when the interface is opened instead: on a
board fed from a marginal supply that keeps the PHY's draw out of the window
where the rail is under the most stress. It also gives the front end a
recovery path it never had, since anything that disturbs it can now be put
back without a reboot; .link_change_notify re-runs the sequence on link loss.

The sequence is the vendor's, in the vendor's order, and reaches the PHY
through the APB alias of its register file rather than over MDIO so it stays
byte-for-byte the known-good one. The MDIO bus lock is held across it because
selecting that alias takes the register file away from the MAC's MDIO master.

Three things follow from the boot loader no longer doing this, and are worth
stating because they are load-bearing:

  * Nothing writes MII_PHYSID1/2 before Linux, so no id can be probed. The
    board DTS declares the PHY with an "ethernet-phy-id0043.5649" compatible
    (patch 0008) and phylib creates the device without reading.
  * .features is stated rather than read. phy_probe() would otherwise call
    genphy_read_abilities(), and at probe the PHY is still in reset and would
    not answer.
  * The wrapper register at base + 0x800 is shared with the mdio-mux-mmioreg
    node. Both map it non-exclusively -- the mux uses a plain ioremap in its
    switch callback -- and both do read-modify-write on disjoint bits, so the
    driver preserves the mux select in bit 7 where the vendor code, having no
    mux driver to disturb, wrote the whole word.

The analog trim is fused per part and the eFUSE is unreadable by the time
Linux runs -- clk_efuse/clk_apb_efuse are modelled by clk-cv1800.c, nothing
claims them, and clk_disable_unused() gates them off at late_initcall_sync --
so U-Boot reads it and publishes it in the device tree (meta-sophgo's 0007).
A fused value is merged into its own field; the vendor's unfused defaults,
used only when the DT carries nothing, replace the register whole. That
asymmetry is the vendor's and is reproduced rather than tidied up.

The defaults are a genuine last resort, not an equivalent path: on a part
that is fused they put the transmitter far enough out of spec that the link
partner drops most frames. A board running on them showed 37 FCS errors in
68 frames received at the switch, and never held a DHCP lease. The driver
warns when it falls back to them.

There is deliberately no .link_change_notify. An earlier revision re-ran the
bring-up on every transition to PHY_NOLINK, which made sense when this driver
only toggled the front-end power enables; now that the sequence ends by
releasing the main reset and restarting autonegotiation, doing that on every
link drop fights phylib's own negotiation. phylib re-runs .config_init after
a reset or resume, which covers the cases that really do lose the front-end
configuration.

Not upstream -- local to this build.
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/phy/Kconfig b/drivers/net/phy/Kconfig
index 98700d0..eaa5d42 100644
--- a/drivers/net/phy/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/Kconfig
@@ -225,6 +225,18 @@ config CORTINA_PHY
 	help
 	  Currently supports the CS4340 phy.
 
+config CV1800B_PHY
+	tristate "Sophgo CV1800B/SG2002 internal Ethernet PHY"
+	depends on ARCH_SOPHGO || COMPILE_TEST
+	help
+	  Supports the 10/100 Ethernet PHY integrated in the Sophgo CV1800B
+	  and SG2002 SoCs.
+
+	  The PHY needs its analog front end powered up explicitly, and loses
+	  that configuration on reset or resume.  Without this driver the
+	  generic PHY driver binds instead and the front end is only ever set
+	  up once, by the boot loader.
+
 config DAVICOM_PHY
 	tristate "Davicom PHYs"
 	help
diff --git a/drivers/net/phy/Makefile b/drivers/net/phy/Makefile
index 76e0db4..4edc156 100644
--- a/drivers/net/phy/Makefile
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/Makefile
@@ -50,6 +50,7 @@ obj-$(CONFIG_BCM_NET_PHYPTP)	+= bcm-phy-ptp.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_BROADCOM_PHY)	+= broadcom.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_CICADA_PHY)	+= cicada.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_CORTINA_PHY)	+= cortina.o
+obj-$(CONFIG_CV1800B_PHY)	+= cv1800b.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_DAVICOM_PHY)	+= davicom.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_DP83640_PHY)	+= dp83640.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_DP83822_PHY)	+= dp83822.o
diff --git a/drivers/net/phy/cv1800b.c b/drivers/net/phy/cv1800b.c
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5e36ee7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/cv1800b.c
@@ -0,0 +1,458 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0+
+/*
+ * Driver for the 10/100 Ethernet PHY integrated in the Sophgo CV1800B /
+ * SG2002 SoCs.
+ *
+ * The PHY comes out of reset shut down, with its analog front end powered off
+ * and untuned, and with no identification (MII_PHYSID1/2 read as zero).  It
+ * needs the CVITEK vendor bring-up sequence before it will train a link at
+ * all: release the shutdown and the digital/analog resets, power the front end
+ * up, apply the analog trim, load the MLT3 / link-pulse / TP_IDLE / 10BaseT
+ * waveform tables, set AGC and the RX equalizer, then release the main reset
+ * to start autonegotiation.
+ *
+ * That sequence traditionally runs in the boot loader.  Doing it here instead
+ * keeps the front end -- line driver, PLL, RX ADC and PGA -- powered down for
+ * the whole of boot, and only turns it on when the interface is actually
+ * brought up.  On a board running from a marginal supply that removes the
+ * PHY's draw from the window where the rail is under the most stress.
+ *
+ * Because the boot loader no longer writes MII_PHYSID1/2, nothing can probe an
+ * id off this PHY: the device tree must declare it with an
+ * "ethernet-phy-id0043.5649" compatible so phylib creates the device without
+ * reading.  For the same reason the driver states .features rather than
+ * letting phylib read the abilities at probe -- at that point the PHY is still
+ * in reset and would not answer.  The first register access of any kind
+ * happens in .config_init, which phylib calls from phy_attach_direct(), i.e.
+ * when the netdev is opened.
+ *
+ * Registers are reached through the APB alias of the PHY register file rather
+ * than over MDIO, so the sequence stays byte-for-byte the vendor's.  The MDIO
+ * bus lock is held across it because switching the alias on takes the register
+ * file away from the MAC's MDIO master.
+ *
+ * Sequence and register values transcribed from the CVITEK vendor U-Boot
+ * (board/cvitek/cv181x/board.c cv181x_ephy_id_init, drivers/net/phy/cvitek.c
+ * cv182xa_ephy_init).  SG2002 is CV181x ("mars"), so the page 18 equalizer
+ * coefficients are the CV181x set.
+ */
+
+#include <linux/bitops.h>
+#include <linux/io.h>
+#include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/of.h>
+#include <linux/phy.h>
+
+/* Written into MII_PHYSID1/2 by the sequence below: ASCII "CVI". */
+#define CV1800B_PHY_ID			0x00435649
+
+/*
+ * Offsets within the mapped EPHY block.  The register file is aliased one
+ * 32-bit word per MII register, so MII register N lives at N * 4.  The two
+ * wrapper registers sit at the top of the same page.
+ */
+#define CV1800B_MII(reg)		((reg) * 4)
+#define CV1800B_CTL			0x800
+#define CV1800B_APB_RW_SEL		0x804
+
+/* CV1800B_APB_RW_SEL */
+#define CV1800B_APB			1	/* register file on the APB alias */
+#define CV1800B_MDIO			0	/* register file back on MDIO   */
+
+/*
+ * CV1800B_CTL, staged: each value releases one more reset.  Bit 7 is not ours
+ * -- it is the mdio-mux-mmioreg select for this same register -- so every
+ * write preserves it.
+ */
+#define CV1800B_CTL_MUX_SEL		BIT(7)
+#define CV1800B_CTL_SHUTDOWN_OFF	0x0900	/* leave shutdown          */
+#define CV1800B_CTL_DIG_RST_N		0x0904	/* + digital reset released */
+#define CV1800B_CTL_ANA_RST_N		0x0906	/* + analog reset released  */
+#define CV1800B_CTL_MAIN_RST_N		0x090e	/* + main reset -> autoneg  */
+
+/* Pages, written to MII register 0x1f as (page << 8). */
+#define CV1800B_PAGE_SEL		0x1f
+#define CV1800B_PAGE_SHIFT		8
+#define CV1800B_PAGE_MAIN		0x00
+#define CV1800B_PAGE_LED		0x01
+#define CV1800B_PAGE_AFE		0x05
+#define CV1800B_PAGE_LINK_PULSE		0x0a
+#define CV1800B_PAGE_TP_IDLE		0x0b
+#define CV1800B_PAGE_10BT_A		0x0d
+#define CV1800B_PAGE_10BT_B		0x0e
+#define CV1800B_PAGE_10BT_C		0x0f
+#define CV1800B_PAGE_MLT3_POS		0x10
+#define CV1800B_PAGE_MLT3_NEG		0x11
+#define CV1800B_PAGE_EQUALIZER		0x12
+#define CV1800B_PAGE_AGC		0x13
+
+/* Page 5: analog front end power-down / enable control. */
+#define CV1800B_PD_EN_CTL		0x10
+#define CV1800B_ANA_PD_OFF		0x0c00
+#define CV1800B_ANA_EN			0x0c7e
+#define CV1800B_EN_TX_RTERM		BIT(0)
+
+/* Page 5: analog trim, and the vendor defaults for an unfused part. */
+#define CV1800B_TX_ITUNE		0x19
+#define CV1800B_TX_ECHO_I		0x15
+#define CV1800B_TX_RTERM		0x16
+#define CV1800B_TX_ITUNE_DEFAULT	0x5a5a
+#define CV1800B_TX_ECHO_I_DEFAULT	0x0000
+#define CV1800B_TX_RTERM_DEFAULT	0x0bb0
+
+/* Fields a fused value occupies; an unfused default writes the register whole. */
+#define CV1800B_TX_ITUNE_MASK		0xffff
+#define CV1800B_TX_ECHO_I_MASK		0xff00
+#define CV1800B_TX_RTERM_MASK		0x0ff0
+#define CV1800B_TRIM_WHOLE_REG		0xffff
+
+#define CV1800B_RX_VCM			0x13
+#define CV1800B_RX_VCM_ADJ		0x0820
+
+struct cv1800b_regval {
+	u8 reg;
+	u16 val;
+};
+
+/*
+ * One analog trim value.  A fused value is merged into its field and the rest
+ * of the register left alone; an unfused default replaces the whole register.
+ * That asymmetry is the vendor's, and is reproduced rather than tidied up.
+ */
+struct cv1800b_trim {
+	u16 val;
+	u16 mask;
+};
+
+struct cv1800b_priv {
+	void __iomem *base;
+	struct cv1800b_trim tx_itune;
+	struct cv1800b_trim tx_echo_i;
+	struct cv1800b_trim tx_rterm;
+};
+
+/* 100BaseT rise/fall shaping, page 5. */
+static const struct cv1800b_regval cv1800b_100bt[] = {
+	{ 0x17, 0x0c10 },	/* rise update            */
+	{ 0x1a, 0x0003 },	/* falling phase          */
+	{ 0x15, 0x0000 },	/* double TX bias current */
+};
+
+/* MLT3 shaping, positive half (page 16) and negative half (page 17). */
+static const struct cv1800b_regval cv1800b_mlt3_pos[] = {
+	{ 0x1a, 0x1000 }, { 0x1b, 0x3020 }, { 0x1c, 0x5040 }, { 0x1d, 0x7060 },
+	{ 0x16, 0x1708 }, { 0x17, 0x3827 }, { 0x18, 0x5748 }, { 0x19, 0x7867 },
+};
+
+static const struct cv1800b_regval cv1800b_mlt3_neg[] = {
+	{ 0x10, 0x9080 }, { 0x11, 0xb0a0 }, { 0x12, 0xd0c0 }, { 0x13, 0xf0e0 },
+	{ 0x14, 0x9788 }, { 0x15, 0xb8a7 }, { 0x16, 0xd7c8 }, { 0x17, 0xf8e7 },
+};
+
+static const struct cv1800b_regval cv1800b_link_pulse[] = {
+	{ 0x10, 0x3e00 }, { 0x11, 0x7864 }, { 0x12, 0x6470 }, { 0x13, 0x5f62 },
+	{ 0x14, 0x5a5a }, { 0x15, 0x5458 }, { 0x16, 0xb23a }, { 0x17, 0x94a0 },
+	{ 0x18, 0x9092 }, { 0x19, 0x8a8e }, { 0x1a, 0x8688 }, { 0x1b, 0x8484 },
+	{ 0x1c, 0x0082 },
+};
+
+static const struct cv1800b_regval cv1800b_tp_idle[] = {
+	{ 0x10, 0x5252 }, { 0x11, 0x5252 }, { 0x12, 0x4b52 }, { 0x13, 0x3d47 },
+	{ 0x14, 0xaa99 }, { 0x15, 0x989e }, { 0x16, 0x9395 }, { 0x17, 0x9091 },
+	{ 0x18, 0x8e8f }, { 0x19, 0x8d8e }, { 0x1a, 0x8c8c }, { 0x1b, 0x8b8b },
+	{ 0x1c, 0x008a },
+};
+
+/* 10BaseT waveform tables, pages 13 through 16. */
+static const struct cv1800b_regval cv1800b_10bt_a[] = {
+	{ 0x10, 0x1e0a }, { 0x11, 0x3862 }, { 0x12, 0x1e62 }, { 0x13, 0x2a08 },
+	{ 0x14, 0x244c }, { 0x15, 0x1a44 }, { 0x16, 0x061c },
+};
+
+static const struct cv1800b_regval cv1800b_10bt_b[] = {
+	{ 0x10, 0x2d30 }, { 0x11, 0x3470 }, { 0x12, 0x0648 }, { 0x13, 0x261c },
+	{ 0x14, 0x3160 }, { 0x15, 0x2d5e },
+};
+
+static const struct cv1800b_regval cv1800b_10bt_c[] = {
+	{ 0x10, 0x2922 }, { 0x11, 0x366e }, { 0x12, 0x0752 }, { 0x13, 0x2556 },
+	{ 0x14, 0x2348 }, { 0x15, 0x0c30 },
+};
+
+static const struct cv1800b_regval cv1800b_10bt_d[] = {
+	{ 0x10, 0x1e08 }, { 0x11, 0x3868 }, { 0x12, 0x1462 }, { 0x13, 0x1a0e },
+	{ 0x14, 0x305e }, { 0x15, 0x2f62 },
+};
+
+static const struct cv1800b_regval cv1800b_agc[] = {
+	{ 0x16, 0x0012 },	/* max swing */
+	{ 0x17, 0x6848 },	/* min swing */
+};
+
+/* RX equalizer, CV181x ("mars") coefficients -- CV180x uses a different set. */
+static const struct cv1800b_regval cv1800b_equalizer[] = {
+	{ 0x12, 0x0808 },	/* lpf */
+	{ 0x13, 0x0808 },
+	{ 0x14, 0x32f8 },	/* hpf */
+	{ 0x15, 0xf8dc },
+};
+
+static void cv1800b_wr(struct cv1800b_priv *priv, u32 off, u16 val)
+{
+	writel(val, priv->base + off);
+}
+
+static u32 cv1800b_rd(struct cv1800b_priv *priv, u32 off)
+{
+	return readl(priv->base + off);
+}
+
+static void cv1800b_mii_wr(struct cv1800b_priv *priv, u8 reg, u16 val)
+{
+	cv1800b_wr(priv, CV1800B_MII(reg), val);
+}
+
+static void cv1800b_mii_setbits(struct cv1800b_priv *priv, u8 reg, u16 clr, u16 set)
+{
+	u32 v = cv1800b_rd(priv, CV1800B_MII(reg));
+
+	cv1800b_wr(priv, CV1800B_MII(reg), (v & ~clr) | set);
+}
+
+static void cv1800b_mii_trim(struct cv1800b_priv *priv, u8 reg,
+			     const struct cv1800b_trim *t)
+{
+	if (t->mask == CV1800B_TRIM_WHOLE_REG)
+		cv1800b_mii_wr(priv, reg, t->val);
+	else
+		cv1800b_mii_setbits(priv, reg, t->mask, t->val & t->mask);
+}
+
+static void cv1800b_select_page(struct cv1800b_priv *priv, u8 page)
+{
+	cv1800b_mii_wr(priv, CV1800B_PAGE_SEL, page << CV1800B_PAGE_SHIFT);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Stage the wrapper reset register.  Bit 7 belongs to the mdio-mux node that
+ * shares this address, so preserve whatever the mux driver left there -- the
+ * vendor code writes the whole word because in the boot loader there is no mux
+ * driver to disturb.
+ */
+static void cv1800b_ctl(struct cv1800b_priv *priv, u16 val)
+{
+	u32 mux = cv1800b_rd(priv, CV1800B_CTL) & CV1800B_CTL_MUX_SEL;
+
+	cv1800b_wr(priv, CV1800B_CTL, val | mux);
+}
+
+static void cv1800b_write_regs(struct cv1800b_priv *priv, u8 page,
+			       const struct cv1800b_regval *regs, size_t n)
+{
+	size_t i;
+
+	cv1800b_select_page(priv, page);
+	for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
+		cv1800b_mii_wr(priv, regs[i].reg, regs[i].val);
+}
+
+#define cv1800b_write_table(priv, page, table) \
+	cv1800b_write_regs((priv), (page), (table), ARRAY_SIZE(table))
+
+/*
+ * The full vendor bring-up.  Ordering matters throughout: the resets are
+ * released in stages around the front-end power-up, the trim has to land
+ * before the waveform tables, and the main reset is released last because that
+ * is what starts autonegotiation.
+ */
+static int cv1800b_config_init(struct phy_device *phydev)
+{
+	struct cv1800b_priv *priv = phydev->priv;
+	unsigned int i;
+
+	/*
+	 * Switching the register file onto the APB alias takes it away from the
+	 * MAC's MDIO master, so hold the bus lock until it is handed back.
+	 */
+	phy_lock_mdio_bus(phydev);
+
+	cv1800b_wr(priv, CV1800B_APB_RW_SEL, CV1800B_APB);
+	cv1800b_ctl(priv, CV1800B_CTL_SHUTDOWN_OFF);
+	cv1800b_ctl(priv, CV1800B_CTL_DIG_RST_N);
+
+	/* Power the analog front end up, then release its reset. */
+	cv1800b_select_page(priv, CV1800B_PAGE_AFE);
+	cv1800b_mii_wr(priv, CV1800B_PD_EN_CTL, CV1800B_ANA_PD_OFF);
+	cv1800b_mii_wr(priv, CV1800B_PD_EN_CTL, CV1800B_ANA_EN);
+	cv1800b_ctl(priv, CV1800B_CTL_ANA_RST_N);
+
+	/*
+	 * Give the PHY an id.  The hardware has none, and without this a later
+	 * probe -- or a boot loader that scans the bus -- finds nothing.
+	 */
+	cv1800b_select_page(priv, CV1800B_PAGE_AFE);
+	cv1800b_mii_wr(priv, MII_PHYSID1, CV1800B_PHY_ID >> 16);
+	cv1800b_mii_wr(priv, MII_PHYSID2, CV1800B_PHY_ID & 0xffff);
+
+	/* Analog trim, then the 100BaseT shaping that follows it. */
+	cv1800b_mii_trim(priv, CV1800B_TX_ITUNE, &priv->tx_itune);
+	cv1800b_mii_trim(priv, CV1800B_TX_ECHO_I, &priv->tx_echo_i);
+	cv1800b_mii_trim(priv, CV1800B_TX_RTERM, &priv->tx_rterm);
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(cv1800b_100bt); i++)
+		cv1800b_mii_wr(priv, cv1800b_100bt[i].reg, cv1800b_100bt[i].val);
+
+	/* MLT3 halves. */
+	cv1800b_write_table(priv, CV1800B_PAGE_MLT3_POS, cv1800b_mlt3_pos);
+	cv1800b_write_table(priv, CV1800B_PAGE_MLT3_NEG, cv1800b_mlt3_neg);
+
+	/* Back to page 5 for the termination and RX common-mode tweaks. */
+	cv1800b_select_page(priv, CV1800B_PAGE_AFE);
+	cv1800b_mii_setbits(priv, CV1800B_PD_EN_CTL, 0, CV1800B_EN_TX_RTERM);
+	cv1800b_mii_setbits(priv, CV1800B_RX_VCM, 0, CV1800B_RX_VCM_ADJ);
+
+	cv1800b_write_table(priv, CV1800B_PAGE_LINK_PULSE, cv1800b_link_pulse);
+	cv1800b_write_table(priv, CV1800B_PAGE_TP_IDLE, cv1800b_tp_idle);
+	cv1800b_write_table(priv, CV1800B_PAGE_10BT_A, cv1800b_10bt_a);
+	cv1800b_write_table(priv, CV1800B_PAGE_10BT_B, cv1800b_10bt_b);
+	cv1800b_write_table(priv, CV1800B_PAGE_10BT_C, cv1800b_10bt_c);
+	/* The fourth 10BaseT block shares page 16 with the MLT3 positive half. */
+	cv1800b_write_table(priv, CV1800B_PAGE_MLT3_POS, cv1800b_10bt_d);
+
+	/* LED_LNK/SPD/DPX onto the LED pad. */
+	cv1800b_select_page(priv, CV1800B_PAGE_LED);
+	cv1800b_mii_setbits(priv, 0x1a, 0x0f00, 0);
+
+	cv1800b_write_table(priv, CV1800B_PAGE_AGC, cv1800b_agc);
+	cv1800b_write_table(priv, CV1800B_PAGE_EQUALIZER, cv1800b_equalizer);
+
+	/* Main reset last: this is what starts autonegotiation. */
+	cv1800b_select_page(priv, CV1800B_PAGE_MAIN);
+	cv1800b_ctl(priv, CV1800B_CTL_MAIN_RST_N);
+	cv1800b_mii_setbits(priv, MII_BMCR, 0, BMCR_FULLDPLX);
+
+	cv1800b_wr(priv, CV1800B_APB_RW_SEL, CV1800B_MDIO);
+
+	phy_unlock_mdio_bus(phydev);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int cv1800b_resume(struct phy_device *phydev)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = genphy_resume(phydev);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	return cv1800b_config_init(phydev);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Read one trim value.  Present in the device tree means fused, so it is
+ * merged into its own field; absent means fall back to the vendor's unfused
+ * default, which replaces the register whole.
+ */
+static void cv1800b_read_trim(struct device_node *np, const char *prop,
+			      struct cv1800b_trim *t, u16 def, u16 mask)
+{
+	u32 val;
+
+	if (np && !of_property_read_u32(np, prop, &val)) {
+		t->val = val;
+		t->mask = mask;
+	} else {
+		t->val = def;
+		t->mask = CV1800B_TRIM_WHOLE_REG;
+	}
+}
+
+static int cv1800b_probe(struct phy_device *phydev)
+{
+	struct device *dev = &phydev->mdio.dev;
+	struct cv1800b_priv *priv;
+	u32 reg[2];
+	int ret;
+
+	priv = devm_kzalloc(dev, sizeof(*priv), GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!priv)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	ret = of_property_read_u32_array(dev->of_node, "sophgo,ephy-reg", reg, 2);
+	if (ret)
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret,
+				     "missing sophgo,ephy-reg (base and size of the EPHY block)\n");
+
+	/*
+	 * Deliberately a plain ioremap and not a managed request: the wrapper
+	 * half of this block is also mapped by the mdio-mux-mmioreg node that
+	 * sits at base + 0x800, which would make an exclusive request fail.
+	 * The two drivers touch disjoint bits of the one register they share.
+	 */
+	priv->base = devm_ioremap(dev, reg[0], reg[1]);
+	if (!priv->base)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	/*
+	 * The trim is fused per part, but the eFUSE is unreadable by the time
+	 * Linux runs -- its clocks are gated off as unused -- so the boot loader
+	 * reads it and publishes the values here.  Falling back to the vendor's
+	 * unfused defaults on a part that is fused produces a transmitter well
+	 * out of spec: the link partner sees a high rate of FCS errors and the
+	 * link does not stay up.  So the defaults are a last resort, and worth a
+	 * warning when they are used.
+	 */
+	cv1800b_read_trim(dev->of_node, "sophgo,tx-itune", &priv->tx_itune,
+			  CV1800B_TX_ITUNE_DEFAULT, CV1800B_TX_ITUNE_MASK);
+	cv1800b_read_trim(dev->of_node, "sophgo,tx-echo-i", &priv->tx_echo_i,
+			  CV1800B_TX_ECHO_I_DEFAULT, CV1800B_TX_ECHO_I_MASK);
+	cv1800b_read_trim(dev->of_node, "sophgo,tx-rterm", &priv->tx_rterm,
+			  CV1800B_TX_RTERM_DEFAULT, CV1800B_TX_RTERM_MASK);
+	if (priv->tx_itune.mask == CV1800B_TRIM_WHOLE_REG &&
+	    priv->tx_rterm.mask == CV1800B_TRIM_WHOLE_REG)
+		phydev_warn(phydev,
+			    "no fused analog trim in the device tree; using defaults, link quality may suffer\n");
+
+	phydev->priv = priv;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static struct phy_driver cv1800b_phy_driver[] = {
+	{
+		PHY_ID_MATCH_EXACT(CV1800B_PHY_ID),
+		.name			= "Sophgo CV1800B internal",
+		/*
+		 * Stated rather than read: at probe the PHY is still in reset
+		 * and would not answer an abilities read.  10/100, the only
+		 * thing this part does.
+		 */
+		.features		= PHY_BASIC_FEATURES,
+		.probe			= cv1800b_probe,
+		.config_init		= cv1800b_config_init,
+		.suspend		= genphy_suspend,
+		.resume			= cv1800b_resume,
+		/*
+		 * No .link_change_notify.  An earlier revision re-ran the
+		 * bring-up on every transition to PHY_NOLINK, from when this
+		 * driver only toggled the front-end power enables.  Now that
+		 * the sequence ends by releasing the main reset and restarting
+		 * autonegotiation, firing it on every link drop fights phylib's
+		 * own negotiation instead of helping it.  phylib already re-runs
+		 * .config_init after a reset or a resume, which covers the cases
+		 * that actually lose the front-end configuration.
+		 */
+	},
+};
+
+module_phy_driver(cv1800b_phy_driver);
+
+static const struct mdio_device_id __maybe_unused cv1800b_phy_tbl[] = {
+	{ PHY_ID_MATCH_EXACT(CV1800B_PHY_ID) },
+	{ }
+};
+
+MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(mdio, cv1800b_phy_tbl);
+
+MODULE_DESCRIPTION("Sophgo CV1800B internal Ethernet PHY driver");
+MODULE_LICENSE("GPL");
